nCase 1: |p+q| = 2. Then sum changes by 2 each step. To have m+1 points, the sum values must be either all even numbers from 2 to 2m+2 (if starting sum is 2) or all odd numbers from 3 to 2m+1 (if starting sum is 3). But the triangle T_{2m+1} includes points with sum from 2 to 2m+2 inclusive. So both possibilities are possible. However, we also need the line to have integer points for each sum value. For each sum s, we need integer solutions (x,y) with x+y = s and satisfying the line equation p x - q y = c. This is a system of two linear equations in integers. For each s, there is a unique solution (x,y) if the determinant is non-zero. Let's solve:\n\nGiven p x - q y = c and x + y = s. Solve for x,y:\n\nFrom x = s - y, substitute: p (s - y) - q y = c => p s - p y - q y = c => p s - (p+q) y = c => y = (p s - c) / (p+q). Since p+q = ±2, denominator is ±2. So y = (p s - c) / (p+q). For integer y, we need p s - c divisible by p+q. Since p+q = ±2, we need p s - c even. Similarly, x = s - y will be integer.\n\nThus for each s, we need parity condition: p s - c ≡ 0 (mod 2). So the parity of s must be such that p s ≡ c (mod 2). Since p is integer, parity of p s is parity of p times parity of s. So if p is odd, then parity of p s = parity of s. If p is even, parity of p s = 0 (even). So the condition reduces to:\n\n- If p is odd: s ≡ c (mod 2). So s must have same parity as c.\n- If p is even: 0 ≡ c (mod 2) => c must be even, and any s works.\n\nThus for lines with p odd, the sum parity is fixed; for p even, any parity works but c must be even.\n\nNow, for |p+q| = 2, p+q = ±2. Let's consider possibilities:\n\n- p = q = 1 => p+q = 2, p odd. Then c must have same parity as s. So s must be odd if c odd, even if c even. For line to intersect T_{2m+1} at points with sum values 2,4,...,2m+2 (all even), we need c even. Then s even works. So line with p=q=1 can have points with even sum values. Indeed, line x - y = c? Wait p x - q y = c => x - y = c. So line x - y = c passes through 